{"record_id":"VCS:3243","description":"Rice cultivation is the most important agricultural operation in Telangana State, not only in terms of food security but also in terms of livelihood and plays a major part in the economy and employment. The agriculture sector is backbone of the rural economy in Telangana. In Telangana State Paddy cultivated is 52,51,261 acres in Vanakalam and 51,71,916 acres in Yasangi and total cultaivated area for the year 2020-21 is 10.423 Million acres . The total production of rice in Telangana was 145.68 lakh tonnes in 2020-21. The district of Suryapet is one of the major producers district of rice. The aim of this project activity is reducing the CH4 emissions by switching traditional rice cultivation to AWD technique. The project activity comprises of grouped projects of rice fields implementing various water management practices located at different areas within the geographical area of India. The proposed project activity instances involve the implementation of the various water management practices to mitigate methane emission from rice fields in regions in Suryapet District in Telangana state, India. View More Units Issued VCUs 0 Active VCUs 0 Retired VCUs 0 Cancelled VCUs 0 Total Buffer Contributions 0","fetched":"2026-10-14"}
{"record_id":"VCS:5859","description":"The AgriCapture Latin America Rice Methane Project will generate emission reductions under VCS Methodology VM0051: Improved Management in Rice Production Systems. The project supports rice growers in Latin America in adopting improved irrigation practices to reduce both greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions and water use. Within the project, growers will implement improved irrigation practices. Prior to project implementation, rice growers in the project practiced continuous flood irrigation. Continuous flooding creates an anaerobic soil environment that fosters methane producing microbes (Searchinger & Waite, 2014). Methane emissions rise as the duration of the flooded period increases, due to the buildup of these microbes (Searchinger & Waite, 2014). By introducing periods of drying that allow oxygen to reenter the soil, improved irrigation practices, like single-drainage and alternate wetting and drying (AWD), can significantly reduce methane emissions. Research indicates that AWD lowers methane emissions by 47% while also decreasing water consumption by up to 39% (Gao, 2024; Atwill R. L., 2020). Participating growers may also transition from transplanted rice to direct seeded rice, another improved irrigation practice. Traditional transplanted rice involves rice seedlings being grown in a nursery before being planted in puddled fields (Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). The process requires a prohibitive amount of labor, and puddling the field requires large amounts of irrigation (Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). Additionally, the puddled fields established before planting increases methane emissions due to increasing the period of anaerobic conditions (Reddy, et al., 2025). When compared to transplanted rice, direct seeded rice reduces methane emissions and water consumption by 70% and 8-12% respectively (Reddy, et al., 2025; Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). View More Units Issued VCUs 0 Active VCUs 0 Retired VCUs 0 Cancelled VCUs 0 Total Buffer Contributions 0","fetched":"2026-10-14"}
{"record_id":"VCS:5890","description":"The AgriCapture South Asia Rice Methane Project will generate emission reductions under VCS Methodology VM0051: Improved Management in Rice Production Systems. The project supports rice growers in South Asia in adopting improved irrigation practices to reduce both greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions and water use. Within the project, growers will implement improved irrigation practices. Prior to project implementation, rice growers in the project practiced continuous flood irrigation. Continuous flooding creates an anaerobic soil environment that fosters methane producing microbes (Searchinger & Waite, 2014). Methane emissions rise as the duration of the flooded period increases, due to the buildup of these microbes (Searchinger & Waite, 2014). By introducing periods of drying that allow oxygen to reenter the soil, improved irrigation practices, like single-drainage and alternate wetting and drying (AWD), can significantly reduce methane emissions. Research indicates that AWD lowers methane emissions by 47% while also decreasing water consumption by up to 39% (Gao, 2024; Atwill R. L., 2020). Participating growers may also transition from transplanted rice to direct seeded rice, another improved irrigation practice. Traditional transplanted rice involves rice seedlings being grown in a nursery before being planted in puddled fields (Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). The process requires a prohibitive amount of labor, and puddling the field requires large amounts of irrigation (Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). Additionally, the puddled fields established before planting increases methane emissions due to increasing the period of anaerobic conditions (Reddy, et al., 2025). When compared to transplanted rice, direct seeded rice reduces methane emissions and water consumption by 70% and 8-12% respectively (Reddy, et al., 2025; Ishfaq, Akbar, Anjum, & Anwar-Ijl-Haq, 2020). View More Units Issued VCUs 0 Active VCUs 0 Retired VCUs 0 Cancelled VCUs 0 Total Buffer Contributions 0","fetched":"2026-10-14"}
{"record_id":"VCS:5987","description":"The Project Activity involves application of Practices which lead to production of water usage, GHG emissions and also focus on improving the yield gradually. This would be achieved by introducing technically proven initiatives/ techniques which would result in reduction of fresh water consumption and thereby decrease the dependency on application of synthetic fertilizers and other amendments. This Project is being developed as a grouped project across multiple states of India. The farmers would start benefiting through reduced the input cost of cultivation, better yields after a certain period of time and reducing the amount of fresh water being used. Environmental benefits include reduction in GHG emissions , less fresh water withdrawal, anaerobic conditions controlled and lesser usage of insecticides and pesticides. Training and support from land preparation to harvesting would be provided to the farmers from the Project Proponent's team. Units Issued VCUs 0 Active VCUs 0 Retired VCUs 0 Cancelled VCUs 0 Total Buffer Contributions 0","fetched":"2026-10-14"}
{"record_id":"VCS:6166","description":"GreenGlobe Agri Venture Pvt Ltd is implementing a project activity that promotes 'Alternate Wetting and Drying' (AWD) and related adjusted water management practices in irrigated lowland rice systems across five selected districts viz. Bhopal, Narmadapuram, Raisen, Sehore and Vidisha, of the state Madhya Pradesh in India. The project activity reduces methane (CH₄) emissions from soil methanogenesis by shifting from continuous flooding to intermittent irrigation, while supporting farmers through training, technical guidance and provision of field water monitoring tools. The project will support farmers through awareness programs, technical training, and the provision of field water tubes to monitor soil water levels, enabling consistent application of AWD. The project is designed as a single project activity with phase-wise implementation of the project activity within a defined geographic boundary. As per the design of the project, the cumulative capacity of the project will be about 43,813 ha, expected to generate average annual GHG emission reductions of approximately 66,905 tCO2e, and approximately 535,243 tCO2e over the initial seven-year crediting period. Project also intends to promote use of direct seeded rice (DSR). Thus, the measure consists of AWD method or AWD in combination with and/or DSR in combination with adjusted water management system in rice cultivation. Overall, the project activity contributes ### GET 429 https://prod-us.api.platts.com/ci-raas-prod/br-reg/rest/public-report-manager/getProjectById/6166/Markit {\"message\":\"API rate limit exceeded\"} ### GET 200 https://prod-us.api.platts.com/ci-raas-prod/cms-resources/public/standardsByRegistry/VERRA [{\"entityName\":null,\"id\":\"150000000000001\",\"metaData\":\"VCS\",\"name\":\"Verified Carbon Standard\",\"publicReportExport\":{ \"ACCOUNTS\": false, \"PROJECTS\": true, \"ISSUANCES_LISTINGS\": true, \"HOLDINGS\": true, \"RETIREMENTS\": true, \"ASSIGNMENTS